    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/process/Process_CodeQualityGates",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns#Process",
      "name": "The gates a change must pass before it can merge",
      "summary": "The gates a change must pass before it can merge: the jobs of the code-quality workflow, each marked hard, warn-only or not a gate, joined by one question — did every hard one pass.",
      "description": "The gates a change must pass before it can merge: the jobs of the code-quality workflow, each marked hard, warn-only or not a gate, joined by one question — did every hard one pass. EVERY GATE IS INDEPENDENT, AND NOTHING IN THE YAML SAYS SO IN ONE PLACE. Bean `7yvd`. The file reads as a long sequence; it is not one. NO gate declares `needs:` on another GATE, so they all start together and the wall-clock cost of the gates is the SLOWEST job, not the sum. The `needs:` in the file are on jobs that are not gates: `qa-publish` (arc `3fva`) waits for the corpus parts of `gates` whatever they concluded and stores the run's QA results on the `qa-reports` branch, and nothing waits for it; and every job that READS the QA working copy waits for `qa-working-copy` (bean `jtfk`), which builds it once for the run instead of once per reader. That is the single most useful thing to know before adding a gate, and it is recoverable today only by checking for `needs:` keys that are not there. COUNT THE `# bpmn-node:` DECLARATIONS rather than trusting a number here: this paragraph said SIX until 2026-09-26, by which point there were eight — `typescript` had been split into `typescript` + `gates` and `skill-registration-chain` added — and the count was wrong in the one place a reader checks the shape.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY IS THE OTHER HALF, AND IT IS THE PART THAT DOES NOT GO STALE. Exactly TWO gates report without blocking — the Rust wildcard report and the dependency advisories, both STEPS of the `hygiene` job since bean `7fu5` — and every other gate is HARD. (`qa-publish` blocks nothing either, but it is not a gate at all, so it is drawn off the join rather than counted here.) Stated that way round on purpose: the warn-only set is named and closed, so it stays true as jobs are added, whereas \"four are hard\" needed editing the moment a ninth gate existed. Drawing them as equal boxes would be a lie a reader would act on, so the warn-only gates are labelled as such and the gateway after the join asks specifically about the HARD ones.\n\nTHE TWO WARN-ONLY GATES ARE WARN-ONLY BY DIFFERENT MECHANISMS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT. The Rust step carries `continue-on-error: true` — the RUNNER discards its result. The advisories step carries no such flag: its script exits 0 in every state and keeps the three states apart in its OUTPUT instead. That is deliberate, because a warn-only step whose exit code says nothing has only its output left to distinguish `found nothing` from `could not find out`, and `continue-on-error` would have thrown away the one signal that still worked.\n\n`continue-on-error` IS NOT A DEFAULT HERE, it is a decision that has been REVERSED once already: the Python import check carried it, and the flag swallowed a failure on a missing path, so the job reported success over a check that never ran. It was removed and a new unused import now fails. The flag STILL survives on exactly one step — adding a second warn-only gate did not add a second `continue-on-error`, which is the history being honoured rather than an oversight. Anyone reaching for the flag should read that paragraph first.\n\n`TypeScript` IS ONE BOX STANDING FOR ROUGHLY THIRTY REPOSITORY GATES — workflow policy, AGENTS.md cross-references and claims, declared assets, workflow skill refs and BPMN coverage, the knowledge-graph audit, generated-docs currency, agent memory, and the ratchet that fails a check script no workflow runs. They are sequential only because one job runs them; each is independent, and `bun run cat gates` is the local runner derived from this job so a contributor can run the same set before pushing.\n\nMechanical throughout. Nothing here waits on a person, which is what makes `build-pipeline` the right lane rather than a convenient one.",
      "enforcement": "strict",
      "sourcePath": "processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn",
      "sourceUrl": "https://github.com/litlfred/cat-harness/blob/main/processes/sdlc/code-quality-gates.bpmn",
      "depiction": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/assets/img/workflows/code-quality-gates.svg",
