        "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns#graphTypology/processes"
      ],
      "description": "Executable BPMN processes and the DMN tables their gateways compute from. The diagrams are the source of truth, not illustrations of one: a lane binds a Role, an activity names the Skill to run through `<folio:skill ref>`, and `workflow_complete` refuses a step that is not enabled. WHERE A RUNNING INSTANCE GOT TO is not here — that is `beans/workflows/`, kind `workflow-state`, which is `state` rather than `content`. Two questions, two graphs. GROUPED BY CONCERN since placement PR3 (bean `63wl`): `processes.json` names the groups, each diagram sits in `processes/<group>/` and a DMN in `processes/<group>/decisions/` beside the diagrams that read it; the content-type diagrams moved up to the instances that own their skills (folio-assistant-core, folio-assistant-sci, fhir-harness), whose same-named `processes/<group>/` are members of these groups.",
      "readmePath": "processes/README.md",
      "fileCount": 0,
      "inSubgraph": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/processes",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/directory/processes"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/methodologies",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns#Subgraph",
      "name": "methodologies",
      "path": "methodologies/",
      "contentSource": {
        "kind": "directory",
        "declaredIn": "default"
      },
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/cat-harness/v0/ns#graphTypology/methodology"
      ],
      "description": "Judgement methodologies the harness owns, one sub-graph each: Kepner-Tregoe for a decision, MADR for its record, DMN for the computable case. PARALLEL rather than composable — which applies is contextual, and blending them produces a house method that cites nobody. Its own graph typology rather than skills, for three properties a skill does not have: extractable (somebody else's work, adopted, so it lifts out with its declaration when the field moves on), referenced rather than inlined (two skills quoting one method is two copies free to drift), and exempt from skill criteria (skill-is-brief caps an INSTRUCTION near 280 lines; a methodology is a faithful rendering of an external standard, and truncating it to fit a house limit would misrepresent the standard). GRADE is deliberately NOT here: it is WHO guideline method, and since 2026-09-24 (bean `wg7r`) it is the `grade` skill in the DAK layer's `skills/content/authoring-who-smart-guidelines/` with its vocabularies as code lists — the owner: \"needs to be part of skill/SKOS etc. … dont want smart-kg here yet, that is its own repo already\". RELOCATION, 2026-09-22, and the THREE declarations it removed. The owner: \"dont bury sub-graph assets. same for <stub>/skills, etc.\" CRDM's three skills moved to `skills/sdlc/crdm/`, RACI's to `skills/process/raci/`, and CRDM's eight diagrams to `processes/` — the stub-level graphs that were already declared. `methodology-crdm`, `methodology-raci` and `methodology-crdm-processes` are GONE rather than repointed: a separate entry for a directory that is already a package subdirectory of the declared `skills/` graph would declare one directory twice, which is the `x4v4` graph-inside-a-graph shape one level down. `gen-skill-docs` demonstrated it rather than anyone reasoning it out — it demanded a category under BOTH the basename and the declaration id, because both discovery branches found the same directory. So this entry now has the `methodology` graph to itself, holding exactly the four nodes and no subgraph: dmn, kepner-tregoe, madr, swot. This REVERSES the placement half of bean `g43o`; its argument that a reader should meet CRDM as a thing rather than as three prefixed filenames is preserved by the package manifest, which is what makes a skill package a thing everywhere else in this tree.",
      "readmePath": "methodologies/README.md",
      "fileCount": 0,
      "inSubgraph": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/methodologies",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/directory/methodologies"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/qa",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns#Subgraph",
      "name": "qa",
      "path": "test/results/",
      "contentSource": {
        "@id": "https://github.com/litlfred/cat-harness/tree/cat/cat-harness/qa-reports",
        "kind": "branch",
        "branch": "cat/cat-harness/qa-reports",
        "keyedBy": "commit",
        "declaredIn": "storage"
      },
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/cat-harness/v0/ns#graphTypology/qa"
      ],
      "description": "Everything a QA process produced, in one declared tree. The owner's rule, 2026-09-19: an artefact generated primarily as a QA reviewer belongs under `test/results/` as part of a QA process — placement follows PROVENANCE, not file family and not who fetches it afterwards. Four kinds live here and the FILES say which they are, per the `$schema` contract: `qa-results/v1` (`<producer>.qa-results.json`, a whole-artefact review — findings about a document the build just produced), `qa-witness/v1` (`witnesses/**`, one per audited subject in the families `block`, `kg` and `translation`, the published projection) `kg-qa/v1` (`kg-qa/**`, the KG verdicts themselves — what `kg-audit` wrote) and `kg-qa-manifest/v1` (`kg-qa.manifest.json`, the auditor's identity for all of them, ONE per instance rather than one per sidecar). The manifest moved here from `skills/` on 2026-09-27: `kg-audit.ts` writes it unconditionally, so auditing every declared instance made its old home CREATE a `skills/` directory holding no skills in each of the six instances that have none. Its registration in `graph-typology-registry.ts` moved from the `skills` kind to `qa` with it — a kind claiming a `$schema` whose files live in another kind's directory is a validator aimed at nothing. The `kg-qa/` tree MIRRORS each subject's path rather than being flat, and that is not tidiness: measured 2026-09-19, four sidecar basenames already occur twice across packages (`editor`, `getting-started`, `idle-backlog`, `l2-dak-authoring`), so a flat directory would silently overwrite four verdicts. `kgQaSidecarPath` in `schemas/sdlc/kg-qa.ts` is the one answer for writer and reader alike. A directory is a PLACE TO LOOK and may hold more than one part of a graph; extension is a coincidence, a declaration inside the file is the contract. ONE entry rather than a nested set, deliberately: #263's own comment names the defect a declaration inside another declaration creates. STILL OUTSIDE: the 122 block verdicts `*.qa.json`, which sit beside their blocks and move next. The witnesses are PUBLISHED at `/assets/qa/`, which is where every badge points; the publishing workflows copy them into `_site/assets/qa/` after Jekyll, since Jekyll builds only `docs/`. Where a file lives and where it is served from are different questions.",
      "fileCount": 0,
      "inSubgraph": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/qa",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/directory/qa"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/attestations",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns#Subgraph",
      "name": "attestations",
      "path": "test/attestations/",
      "contentSource": {
        "kind": "directory",
        "declaredIn": "default"
      },
      "holdsGraph": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/cat-harness/v0/ns#graphTypology/attestations"
      ],
      "description": "The JUDGEMENT half of every QA verdict this instance carries across runs, split out of `test/results/` by owner ruling D2 (a), 2026-10-01 (bean `2gst`, arc `3fva`): derived script verdicts move to the `qa-reports` branch; anything a reviewer judged stays HERE, on main, where a `git rm` of derived results cannot reach it. One `qa-attestations/v1` file per subject at `<family>/<mirrored subject path>.attestations.json`, where `<family>` is the derived family it sits beside (`kg-qa`, bean `2gst`; `block-qa` and `translation-qa`, bean `8wj1`) and the mirror is exactly that family's own, so a subject's attestation is found by swapping the tree root and the suffix. Today: the `pair_attestations` that were carried inside `kg-qa/v1` sidecars, ALL of them, the `by: baseline` entries with the `by: agent` ones; the owner ruled on 2026-10-01 that a baseline is a judgement, because a regenerated one forgets the drift it recorded (defect C4). `voice_reviews` live here too when one is recorded. Under `block-qa/` and `translation-qa/`: every block or translation verdict whose `reviewer.kind` is not `script` (13 at the split, 11 + 2), held in a `criteria` map of the derived report's own shape; the derived report keeps a projection composed from here. Schema and the five read states (hit / miss / absent / corrupt / unknown): `schemas/sdlc/qa-attestations.ts`. Owner ruling 2 (2026-10-01): a writer that finds judgements in a prior derived file and no store entry MOVES them here as it saves, rather than refusing; a corrupt store is still unknown and refused. Hosted instances (bootstrap) would sit under `<stub>/`, as their `qa` verdicts do. Declared WITH its files, per bean dh4f.",
      "readmePath": "test/attestations/README.md",
      "fileCount": 0,
      "inSubgraph": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/STAGING/claude-staging-content-change-review-phases/cat-harness/directory/attestations",
      "alternateOf": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/directory/attestations"
    },
    {
